INT. HOSPITAL WAITING ROOM - NIGHT

Vinyl chairs bolted in rows. A vending machine glowing. A muted TV showing a cooking competition.

GLORIA PEMBERTON, 60s, in a church dress and orthopedic shoes, sits bolt upright, purse on her lap, rosary twisted around her fingers. Next to her, WALTER PEMBERTON, 60s, in a windbreaker, stares at a single spot on the carpet. A coffee cup in his hand has gone cold.

A phone rings on the reception desk, unattended. Neither of them moves. It rings five times. Stops.

GLORIA
Walter.

WALTER
Yes.

GLORIA
Say something.

WALTER
What do you want me to say?

GLORIA
Anything.

WALTER
It's a long night.

GLORIA
That's something.

Silence. On the TV, a chef flips an omelet. Gloria's rosary beads click. Walter shifts his weight, sets the cup on the floor, picks it up again.

The intercom crackles overhead.

DR. ESCOBEDO (V.O.)
(over speaker, tinny)
Family of Pemberton, please come to Room 4.

They both rise at once, bump shoulders, step back. Gloria goes first. Walter hesitates, drops his cup in the trash, and grabs her arm, and they stand there a moment, staring at the closed door at the end of the corridor, her fingers digging into his sleeve while his hand covers hers, neither of them able to take the first step, until Gloria whispers a prayer, takes a breath, and moves, and Walter follows a half-step behind her, the way he has for forty years.

DR. ESCOBEDO (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Mr. and Mrs. Pemberton. Take your time.

GLORIA
(stopping)
No. Not take my time.

WALTER
Gloria.

GLORIA
If I take my time, I'll turn around.

WALTER
Then we don't take our time.

He squeezes her hand. She nods. They walk. At the door, she grips the handle, pauses, and pushes.
